CORE no-new-smt broken-smt-backend wide-pointer-encoding
main.c
--wide-pointer-encoding --arch none --little-endian
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] .* assertion u\.p == 0: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
With --arch none the null pointer is not guaranteed to be the all-zero
bit pattern (config.ansi_c.NULL_is_zero is false). Because the wide
pointer encoding keeps the integer address separate from the
object/offset encoding, a pointer whose bytes are all zero -- here
obtained by type-punning the integer 0 through a union -- is not treated
as the null pointer, so `u.p == 0` is not provable.

This is in contrast to the default encoding (see no-arch.desc, tagged
no-wide-pointer-encoding), which reuses the object/offset bits as the
integer address and therefore reports VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL here.
